{"status":"question","question":{"text":"Before I recommend a stack, one thing shapes everything: how do you and your partner actually want to reach this — a small web app you open in a browser (my recommendation), or a local desktop tool that runs on one machine? I'd lean web app: two people means two devices, and a browser app is the simplest way for you both to get to the same data without installing anything. That said, if the data living purely on your own laptop matters more to you than shared access, a local tool is the other honest option. Which fits how you two will use it?","choices":["Web app you both open in a browser","Local desktop/CLI tool on one machine"],"recommended":"Web app you both open in a browser","closing":false},"prd":"# Mission: Budget & expense tracker — foundation\n\n**Type:** feature\n**Goal:** Stand up a running skeleton, a working test system, and one thin vertical slice proving both are connected — for a budget & expense tracker.\n**Acceptance Criteria:**\n- (to be assembled during Align)\n**Out of Scope:**\n- Budgets (deferred to a later mission)\n- Charts (deferred to a later mission)\n- Bank sync (deferred to a later mission)\n**Constraints:**\n- Foundation mission only: skeleton + test runner + one vertical slice; the product itself is not built here.\n"}

